CASE = [
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> Multi-purpose river valley projects were launched after Independence with an integrated water resources management approach. For example, in the Sutlej-Beas river basin the Bhakra-Nangal project water is used both for hydel power production and irrigation, and the Hirakud project in the Mahanadi basin integrates conservation of water with flood control. "
   r"(i) What is a multi-purpose river project? (ii) Name the river on which the Hirakud project is built. (iii) Name one use of the Bhakra-Nangal project. (iv) How do dams harm rivers and the people living near them?",
   r"(i) A project serving many purposes together such as irrigation, power generation, flood control and water supply (1). (ii) Mahanadi (1). (iii) Hydel power production or irrigation (1). (iv) Damming affects natural flow, causes excessive sedimentation in the reservoir and fragments rivers, harming aquatic life; it also displaces local communities and can increase floods (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> In the semi-arid and arid regions of Rajasthan, particularly Bikaner, Phalodi and Barmer, almost all houses traditionally had underground tanks or tankas for storing drinking water. Rooftop rainwater was collected in them and, in some houses, tanks were as large as a big room. Today, many households have shifted to piped water from the Indira Gandhi Canal. "
   r"(i) What are tankas? (ii) Which water was stored in them? (iii) Name the region of Rajasthan mentioned in the passage. (iv) Why is rainwater harvesting important in arid regions?",
   r"(i) Underground tanks for storing drinking water (1). (ii) Rainwater collected from rooftops (1). (iii) Bikaner, Phalodi or Barmer (any one) (1). (iv) Rainfall is scanty and uncertain, groundwater is scarce, so harvesting rain gives a safe local source of water throughout the year and reduces dependence on distant sources (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> Although three-fourths of the world is covered with water and water is renewable through the hydrological cycle, many regions suffer from water scarcity. In India scarcity is caused by a large and growing population, unequal access to water, over-exploitation of groundwater, expanding irrigation and industries using freshwater, and pollution of existing water. "
   r"(i) Which cycle makes water a renewable resource? (ii) Give any one cause of water scarcity mentioned. (iii) Name one traditional method of irrigation used in the hills. (iv) Suggest two ways in which a household can conserve water.",
   r"(i) Hydrological (water) cycle (1). (ii) Population growth, over-exploitation, unequal access or pollution (any one) (1). (iii) Kuls (guls) or bamboo drip irrigation (1). (iv) Rooftop rainwater harvesting; fixing leaking taps; reusing water for gardens (any two) (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
]

